00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.471
your background and stuff?

00:03:09.471 --> 00:03:11.220
ANDY OXFELD: So I'm
an engineering manager

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.540
at Humble Bundle.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.770
So I've been with the company
for almost four years,

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.550
and I've done a little
bit of everything.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.800
I've worked on pretty much
every part of the code base

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.542
at some point, and I
originally have my background

00:03:22.542 --> 00:03:23.500
in the gaming industry.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.410
I worked at Electronic
Arts for a number of years

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.701
before coming to Humble Bundle.

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:29.022
So even though I'm not
working on games anymore,

00:03:29.022 --> 00:03:30.480
I have a lot of
familiarity, and it

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.410
helps a lot, with our
mission as a company being

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:34.930
to support game developers.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:35.680
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.830
So obviously we talked just a
little bit there about games.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.170
Tell us what Humble Bundle
is, like it's history.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.550
It's changed a bit over
the last few years,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.730
but I was definitely around
when it first came out,

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.800
and I was really excited by it.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.610
So tell us a little
bit about what it is.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.670
ANDY OXFELD: So Humble
Bundle was actually

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.180
started by game developers.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.770
So our two founders,
Jeff and John,

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.990
were originally working
at Wolfire games,

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.220
and they were creating
a ninja rabbit fighting

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.432
game called Overgrowth.

00:03:59.432 --> 00:04:01.640
That company still exists,
and they're actually doing

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.300
really awesome things there.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.270
But Humble Bundle came
out of a promotion

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.500
that Wolfire games was doing.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.180
And they basically
wanted to create

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.280
kind of the most humble
promotion, hence the name.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.800
And they decided,
what would be more

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.130
humble than being
cross-platform, completely

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.320
DRM free, and letting
the customer pay whatever

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.650
they want?

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.570
And so Humble Bundle
came up with a promotion

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.210
between-- included games
from Wolfire games as well as

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:31.230
a few other developers
and put them together

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.680
into the first Humble Bundle.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.500
And it was so wildly successful
that our founders realized,

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.950
we should try this again.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.260
And the second one was
also wildly successful.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.410
And so they decided to split
it off into its own company.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.820
And since then, really the
mission of Humble Bundle

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.620
has been to support
game developers

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.580
and create cool promotions
for the community.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.160
MARK MANDEL: And not only did
it include pay what you want,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.539
but also it included, you
could do donations to charity,

00:04:55.539 --> 00:04:56.830
and also do that sort of stuff.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:57.300
ANDY OXFELD: Absolutely.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.810
So charity has always been an
important part of our mission,

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.180
and every project we've
created has some way

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.400
in which we support charity.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.321
And today we've raised more
than $70 million for charity,

00:05:06.321 --> 00:05:08.070
and that's something
we're really proud of

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.180
and something that we're going
to continue to do, and really

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:10.831
take to heart.

00:05:10.831 --> 00:05:11.580
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.080
Excellent.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.580
Now in the beginning, I
know it was very one-offish,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.830
like you had these one
promotions and stuff like that,

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.140
though I know things are a
little bit different now.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.806
Do you want to talk about how is
that different now versus then?

00:05:21.806 --> 00:05:23.860
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah, so as
we grew and scaled up,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.520
we started from a bundle would
come out every few months.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.260
We'd have pretty long gaps
where there was almost nothing

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:30.940
on our website.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.770
Existing customers
could come to it

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.520
to download the things
they had purchased,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.660
but otherwise it's
nothing to do there.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.370
And it was really a
big deal, I remember,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.369
the first time that we
had back-to-back bundles,

00:05:41.369 --> 00:05:43.910
where it was like, really, oh,
we've never tried this before.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:44.493
This is crazy.

00:05:44.493 --> 00:05:46.700
It was years ago, so now
it seems kind of the norm.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.730
So now we're at the point we
have multiple bundles running

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:51.680
at the same time, and
we have other products

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.670
that we've created as well.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.211
So now there's always tons of
things going on on our website.

00:05:55.211 --> 00:05:57.390
But back where we were
kind of getting started,

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.930
things were definitely a lot
more sparse and a lot less

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.014
regular than they are now.

00:06:02.014 --> 00:06:03.596
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
when you're saying

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:05.580
that you were very
successful at the beginning,

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:10.360
like every single bundle was a
wild success, how many people

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.120
accessing the
website, more or less?

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.744
What are we talking about?

00:06:13.744 --> 00:06:15.410
ANDY OXFELD: In terms
of actual numbers,

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.900
I couldn't say numbers
actually accessing.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.580
But I do know that we
were regularly seeing up

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.840
to a million purchasers
for our bundles,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.130
and you'll continue to
see numbers like that.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:29.657
Thinking about how I'm sure
many, many people always

00:06:29.657 --> 00:06:31.240
visited and don't
purchase, so I think

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.510
we're almost definitely
looking at millions of visits,

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.190
but I couldn't give
you the exact number.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:35.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:36.420
MARK MANDEL: It's
actually really cool

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.530
if you go on the website,
they do have the stats

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:39.644
at the bottom of the page.

00:06:39.644 --> 00:06:40.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What?

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.290
MARK MANDEL: That show
you total payments

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.589
split by operating system,
number of purchases.

00:06:45.589 --> 00:06:47.130
I've been a longtime
Linux user, so I

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.970
used to go back on a
regular basis and be like,

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.170
hey, are Linux users paying more
than Windows users and stuff

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.220
like that, and actually
look at the numbers.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.416
So a lot of that
stuff really exists,

00:06:55.416 --> 00:06:57.290
and they highlight top
contributors and stuff

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:57.550
like that.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.190
It's a pretty awesome
thing that you're doing.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:06:59.680
ANDY OXFELD: Thanks.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.388
MARK MANDEL: So you
said you were dealing

00:07:01.388 --> 00:07:04.320
with millions and millions
of-- millions at the very least

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.977
of people buying products.

00:07:05.977 --> 00:07:07.810
Let's talk a little bit
about the technology

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.080
that powers Humble Bundle
and what's going on there.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.970
I believe you people
are using GCP.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.190
ANDY OXFELD: Uh-huh,
that's correct.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.010
We've been using App
Engine since day one,

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:20.610
and over time we've continued
to expand our usage of Google

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.700
Cloud Platform offerings.

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:24.020
And we're now using
Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.890
We're using BigQuery and
starting to use Google Compute

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:27.530
Engine as well.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.420
And those have definitely
been critical to our success.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:30.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.464
So could you tell us
a little bit more?

00:07:32.464 --> 00:07:35.150
Why did you choose App
Engine at the beginning?

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:37.720
ANDY OXFELD: So we knew
that, as you mentioned,

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.370
our traffic was going
to be very spiky.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:43.640
So the automatic
scaling of App Engine

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.380
was absolutely crucial
for our success,

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.042
and I don't think
the company really

00:07:47.042 --> 00:07:48.250
could have worked without it.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.100
So the fact that
there's basically--

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.020
without automatic
scaling, we could

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.240
have had-- if we had a
fixed number of servers,

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.156
there's two different
ways it could have gone.

00:07:58.156 --> 00:08:00.180
So you could have either
had too few servers

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.630
and customers want to
come to our website,

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.940
things would have been slow
or wouldn't have worked,

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.970
and customers wouldn't have been
able to make all the purchases

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:07.960
they were able to make.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.480
Or we would have had enough
servers to meet the demand,

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.670
but that would mean
that as soon as-- really

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.770
the first few hours of a bundle
have always been, by far,

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.270
when most of the
traffic comes in.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.460
But the bundle runs for
two weeks, and by the end

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.130
it's more kind of a
trickle of purchases

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.330
compared to the beginning.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:24.420
So we were paying
for all the service

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.710
that we needed to support
those initial few hours

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.200
at the end of the
bundle, our costs

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.700
would have been so
high that there's

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.660
no way the company could
have been profitable.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.860
So the fact that App
Engine, without any thought

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.585
on our part, was able to
scale up and scale down

00:08:36.585 --> 00:08:37.960
as we needed, we
didn't even have

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.400
to think about it, especially
when the company was really

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:44.880
small and we didn't have a big
team, it just allowed us to get

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.150
started and just focus on
selling games and doing

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:49.980
cool promotions and
not worrying about all

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:51.874
the little nitty-gritty
details of the tech.

00:08:51.874 --> 00:08:52.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.540
So knowing that
Humble Bundle has

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.850
been out there for
quite a long time,

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.249
I don't think you're using Go.

00:08:59.249 --> 00:09:00.540
So what language are you using?

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.290
ANDY OXFELD: So
we're using Python.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.740
That was-- if I were
to do it all again,

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.230
I actually might
consider using Go.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.820
I do like a lot of
things about Go.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.710
But at this point,
I think we're pretty

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:12.017
much-- have so much
Python code written,

00:09:12.017 --> 00:09:13.600
I think that's what
we're going to be.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.990
Can you tell us a little bit
more about what parts of App

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:17.490
Engine?

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.350
Like App Engine is
basically an ecosystem.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.160
There's a bunch
of things inside.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:21.910
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:25.330
So we definitely make extensive
use of the Cloud Datastore.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.980
So the performance and
scalability of the data store

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.770
still amazes me to this day.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.300
It's still almost
like magic to me

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:34.220
that this thing is able to work.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:37.830
So I think we've over time
become-- I kind of describe us

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.380
as Datastore wizards, and
we know the ins and outs

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.510
of everything you can do
with it, and every trick

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:47.470
and how to-- both from how
to make things really fast

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.510
and how to keep costs
down and stuff like that.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:52.542
So that's definitely a really
big part of our platform.

00:09:52.542 --> 00:09:54.410
Task queues are also
really important to us,

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:55.834
so we use task queues.

00:09:55.834 --> 00:09:57.500
The most crucial thing
that goes through

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.670
is our payment
processing is completely

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:00.570
based on task queues.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.000
But we also use tasks
for different things,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.850
like when we have something
that we want to schedule.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:09.680
So for example, we often
will at like 10 AM every day

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:11.660
add new products onto
our store, and we want

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.160
to schedule it to be at 10 AM.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.070
We used to have to have
someone that was there and had

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:17.550
to push a button.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.330
But now, with task queues,
we can just schedule an event

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:21.872
and we just know
it's going to run.

00:10:21.872 --> 00:10:24.440
And that's especially
important because since we

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.966
sell international
products, sometimes we

00:10:26.966 --> 00:10:28.840
have so many time zones
such that people used

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.850
to have to be awake
at 1 AM in the morning

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.480
our time zone to launch
something in another time zone.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.938
But now we just schedule it
and we know it's going to work,

00:10:35.938 --> 00:10:36.892
and it just goes.

00:10:36.892 --> 00:10:39.350
MARK MANDEL: So you're able to
leverage the ability of task

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.420
queues to retry if something
fails, and so you're like,

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.900
I know this is going
to work at some point.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:44.070
This is going to be fine.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:44.780
ANDY OXFELD: Absolutely.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:45.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:46.534
That's actually
very interesting,

00:10:46.534 --> 00:10:50.910
the fact that you're using task
queues for the payment system.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:52.972
Payments are a very
tricky thing to do.

00:10:52.972 --> 00:10:54.430
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.640
about how you
manage that exactly?

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.390
And also, what are you using
for the payments themselves?

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:00.098
ANDY OXFELD: Yes.

00:11:00.098 --> 00:11:02.440
So the real thing we use
it for is IPN handling.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.030
So we don't actually do the
direct payment processing

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:05.530
ourselves.

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:06.960
We use PayPal.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.032
We use Amazon, we use
Stripe, and we use

00:11:10.032 --> 00:11:11.240
Coinbase and a couple others.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:17.570
So early on, we found that
if we receive an IPN from one

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:20.170
of them-- so IPN stands for
instant payment notification,

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.410
and it's the main way in
which a company like PayPal

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:24.870
communicates.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.190
It's almost like an API they
use to communicate with us.

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:32.530
And so sometimes, we
need the reliability

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.900
of knowing when an IPN
comes in that it's always

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:36.070
going to be processed.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:39.070
So what we do now is when
an IPN-- when we essentially

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:42.260
receive a request from
PayPal saying, hey, here's

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:44.370
some data we need you
to process, what we do

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.870
is we immediately
write an entity

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:48.190
to the datastore containing
the contents of that IPN,

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.060
and then we schedule a
task to process that.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:54.050
And so we know that no matter
what goes wrong, whether it's

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:56.200
sometimes an engineer on
our team makes a mistake

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.490
and there's a bug in
the code and it crashes,

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:01.640
we know that we're not
going to lose that data.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.300
We can look at our
logs and figure out

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:06.240
exactly what went wrong and fix
the mistake and retry the task,

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:09.947
and ultimately we
can be confident

00:12:09.947 --> 00:12:11.280
that it's going to be processed.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.738
And that's really
important for us,

00:12:12.738 --> 00:12:14.760
since we have really
complicated accounting,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.520
since a big part of
what we actually do

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.560
is moving money to
different places.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.450
So we need to make sure that
our developers, charities,

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:23.960
customers, can trust
us to make sure

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.639
that every penny
that we receive is

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:26.930
going to go to the right place.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.400
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that seems
really important, especially

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.774
with the pay what
you want model,

00:12:29.774 --> 00:12:32.247
and the customers can choose
which companies get what,

00:12:32.247 --> 00:12:34.330
and what charities get
what, and it's percentages.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:37.127
So yeah, it's not as simple as
that thing's $10 and thing's 5,

00:12:37.127 --> 00:12:38.710
and we're going to
receive everything.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:39.980
ANDY OXFELD: Exactly.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:40.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.352
So you mentioned that
you're using App Engine.

00:12:42.352 --> 00:12:43.310
You're using Datastore.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:46.290
You're using task queues.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.900
What else are you
using from App Engine?

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.510
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah, let's see--

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:50.980
MARK MANDEL: I think you said--

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.188
ANDY OXFELD: We use Memcache.

00:12:52.188 --> 00:12:55.000
So Memcache is incredibly
important for performance

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.270
for us, since we want
our site to load quickly.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.520
And Memcache also helps
us cut down on our costs,

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.799
since accessing the
datastore directly costs

00:13:02.799 --> 00:13:04.090
more money than using Memcache.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.230
We actually recently switched
to dedicated Memcache,

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:10.310
which we found actually, despite
the fact that it costs money,

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:12.620
in the end it actually
brought down our costs because

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.400
of the fact that I think
it actually decreased

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.170
other usages, front-end instance
hours and things like that,

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:22.502
to the point where it almost
certainly saves us more money

00:13:22.502 --> 00:13:23.710
than we actually spent on it.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:25.390
So that was
definitely a big win.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:27.005
And not only that,
but that of course

00:13:27.005 --> 00:13:28.630
makes our site even
faster, since we're

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.210
able to store more
data in the cache.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:30.880
MARK MANDEL: So
you were actually

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.480
using the shared Memcache
instance for a long time.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:34.710
ANDY OXFELD: We were using
that for quite a long time.

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.510
MARK MANDEL: And so you'd get
kicked out every so often.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:37.370
You'd get your data kicked out.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:37.800
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.220
And the other thing
about the shared

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.570
is that over time, it kind
of varies how much you have.

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:43.620
So there's times when
your performance is better

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.703
and times when it's worse.

00:13:44.703 --> 00:13:47.450
But with dedicated Memcache,
it's very predictable.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:48.790
MARK MANDEL: Very, very cool.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:51.300
Now, I think you also said you
started using BigQuery a bit

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:52.485
as well.

00:13:52.485 --> 00:13:53.860
Talk us a little
bit through what

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.850
made you make that decision
and what you're doing with it.

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.410
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah,
so we originally

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:01.990
started using BigQuery
for our accounting system.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:08.690
So we used to, every month-- so
we pay developers and charities

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.180
that sell through us.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.330
We cut them a check
every month for however

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.730
much that we owe them.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:17.032
And then we used to have
this complicated map job,

00:14:17.032 --> 00:14:18.490
essentially, where
we would go over

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:20.440
every order in the datastore.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:23.820
And then we realized over
time that that was not

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:24.860
scaling to our needs.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.640
And so what we now
do is we leverage

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:31.350
the automated backup technology
that App Engine provides

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:34.690
to back up from-- I think
it uses Google Cloud

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:36.440
Storage as an intermediary,
but in the end

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.200
it ends up in BigQuery.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:39.325
And so once we have all
our order data in BigQuery,

00:14:39.325 --> 00:14:43.330
we're able to do really powerful
analysis on our order data

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:43.930
in seconds.

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:47.740
And so we have kind of a
complicated set of BigQuery

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:49.990
queries that we've essentially
built over time that we

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:51.198
kind of run and put together.

00:14:51.198 --> 00:14:54.310
And basically now it's a
fairly automated process

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.825
by which it now actually
every day kind of spits out,

00:14:56.825 --> 00:14:58.700
OK, well, here's how
much money everyone that

00:14:58.700 --> 00:14:59.834
sells through here is owed.

00:14:59.834 --> 00:15:01.500
We don't really have
to worry about-- it

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.082
used to take hours and hours.

00:15:03.082 --> 00:15:03.790
Now it's seconds.

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:06.175
So that's been a really,
really great boon for us.

00:15:06.175 --> 00:15:07.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.630
So then, what you said
is that you're actually

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.600
dumping the whole
datastore to Cloud Storage,

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.890
and then uploading
it back to BigQuery

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:16.390
so you can do all your queries.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:16.890
Nice.

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:17.640
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.320
And so we also-- in
addition to that,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:20.760
we also have been
taking advantage

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:21.870
of streaming in BigQuery.

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.980
So we also stream
our data as well.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.940
And we like to use both,
because we can kind of

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.830
check for discrepancies
and just be

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:33.737
100% confident that every order
is represented in BigQuery.

00:15:33.737 --> 00:15:35.070
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty cool.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.890
So basically you're
taking advantage of two

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:39.910
of our big managed services.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.615
So I'm guessing you still
don't have much of an ops team

00:15:42.615 --> 00:15:43.240
at the company.

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:43.600
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:44.890
ANDY OXFELD: So we have
no ops team at all.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:45.260
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.410
ANDY OXFELD: We completely
are able to rely on the Cloud

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:47.910
Platform.

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.580
And again, that's wonderful,
because just to be honest,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:51.800
none of us wanted to do ops.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:53.081
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:53.081 --> 00:15:54.440
Not what our background's in.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:57.729
And so it lets us
focus on writing code.

00:15:57.729 --> 00:15:59.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is a very good point.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:03.520
So what else do you use for--
so you mentioned Cloud Storage.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.170
You use it for temporary
backup for datastore.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:09.200
Do you use it something
else, like your images

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:10.550
or some binaries or whatever?

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:11.490
ANDY OXFELD: Yep.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:14.070
Our other big thing is serving
images, as you mentioned.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.210
So we've actually
started building

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:19.140
a suite of internal
tools around it

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:21.810
that allows-- so we
have a team internally

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:24.610
that makes sure all
the image assets

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.360
for the various
promotions we put together

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:27.420
are in the right place.

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.330
So they'll offload all the
assets into Google Cloud

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:33.760
Storage, and so essentially what
our engineering team has built

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.870
is essentially
kind of like a CMS,

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.582
like a content management system
for putting bundles together,

00:16:38.582 --> 00:16:40.790
that another team in our
company uses to actually put

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:41.665
the bundles together.

00:16:41.665 --> 00:16:43.620
And so Google Cloud
Storage allows

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.532
us to do this,
because originally,

00:16:45.532 --> 00:16:47.740
when we were kind of tiny
and when our engineers used

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:51.410
to build every bundle by
hand, and then every time we

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.370
wanted to change what
bundle was active,

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:55.590
we had to deploy the site.

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:58.330
But now that we're able to use
Google Cloud Storage to host

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:00.350
the images, we can just upload
the images to Google Cloud

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:02.350
Storage, reference the
images from the live site

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:03.410
and not have to deploy.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:06.176
So that's been a really
big win for us as well.

00:17:06.176 --> 00:17:07.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.349
So since you
mentioned deploying,

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:10.829
how often do you deploy?

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:12.579
ANDY OXFELD: Probably
like 10 times a day.

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:12.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:13.109
ANDY OXFELD: Sometimes more.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:13.880
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:15.630
Is that actually
code deployments,

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.380
or just changes to
the database, or both?

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:18.760
ANDY OXFELD: Code deployments.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
How many modules do

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:21.930
you have, or it's
just one big module?

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:25.820
ANDY OXFELD: So we have almost--
probably 95% of our code base

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:26.660
is in one module.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:29.990
Then we have a few
other tinier modules.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:35.575
The most notable one is we've
built a uploader, which uses--

00:17:35.575 --> 00:17:39.670
or CDN, which is-- a
separate company does that.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:41.950
So we've built an uploader
that the uploader does

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:45.970
run in App Engine, and so
that is a separate module that

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:48.670
is kind of a very isolated
problem we wanted to solve.

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.580
And so that's able to
live separately and use

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:52.310
different technologies,
which is nice.

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:54.580
MARK MANDEL: Do you store
the binaries for the games,

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:56.335
or is that done
by a third party?

00:17:56.335 --> 00:17:57.520
What's the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:17:57.520 --> 00:17:58.710
ANDY OXFELD: We do
store the binaries,

00:17:58.710 --> 00:17:59.920
and that's what the CDN does.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.160
And that's what this uploader
module does is uploads

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:03.960
the binaries to our CDN.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.850
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
curious, what would you

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:08.672
consider probably the biggest
challenge you've encountered

00:18:08.672 --> 00:18:10.880
when building Humble Bundle
working with Google Cloud

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:11.490
Platform?

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.198
What's been sort of
the trickiest problem

00:18:13.198 --> 00:18:14.371
that you've come across?

00:18:14.371 --> 00:18:15.870
ANDY OXFELD:
Definitely scalability,

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:17.790
although App Engine's
tools definitely

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:20.420
provide solutions to that.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:22.820
It comes down to
often we'll write

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:27.420
our code-- problem one is always
just get the code working.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.440
And so for example, when we
built the Humble store, which

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.350
is a bit of a more
traditional storefront

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:34.600
that, unlike our bundles,
which are pay what you want,

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:36.330
the Humble store is
fixed-price sales.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:38.580
When we first built
that, we put together

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:40.350
in just a couple weeks.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.296
Then at the time, we
only had 10 games on it.

00:18:42.296 --> 00:18:43.670
So the original
solution we built

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:45.590
for it was not scalable at all.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:48.690
We were actually dumping the
entire inventory directly

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:51.180
to the browser.

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:53.030
So at first, it
loaded pretty fast.

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:55.410
When we hit 50 games, it
started to be a little slower.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.850
Once we had 100
games on the store,

00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.850
the store was taking
about 10 seconds to load,

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.780
and we realized we were going to
have to use a better approach.

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:04.669
And so we looked
at the technologies

00:19:04.669 --> 00:19:07.210
App Engine provided, and we were
able to leverage task queues

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:10.420
and Memcache and build a
more sophisticated system

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:13.740
that now we do a much smarter
approach, where we only

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:18.210
send the games that the
browser needs to know about.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:20.410
And so now we have
thousands and thousands

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:23.730
of games on our store, and
the store loads very fast.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:24.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:27.740
And something that our
customers very often wonder

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:31.334
is, how do you monitor
all these systems?

00:19:31.334 --> 00:19:33.500
ANDY OXFELD: So we have a
number of ways we do that.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:36.370
So we have a bunch of things
we've kind of built internally

00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:38.900
on top of the log
system in App Engine.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:43.630
So we have Cron jobs that
run every few minutes that

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:46.880
check the logs, look for
exceptions and stuff like that.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:48.850
If they're above a
certain threshold,

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:52.090
they'll actually ping
our phones, occasionally

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:53.354
in the middle the night.

00:19:53.354 --> 00:19:56.310
But it lets us know
when things go wrong.

00:19:56.310 --> 00:20:01.900
And we also have dashboards
that we look at, some of which

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:02.770
we built by hand.

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:06.340
I actually check our App
Engine usage every day

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.800
just to make sure if our
numbers are going too high,

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:11.970
in case an engineer
actually deployed

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.129
some code that's doing
tons and tons of datastore

00:20:14.129 --> 00:20:15.170
reads that we don't want.

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:18.894
That way we catch it before our
bill ends up going too high.

00:20:18.894 --> 00:20:20.310
More recently,
we've been starting

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.851
to look into some of the
new Stackdriver things.

00:20:22.851 --> 00:20:25.350
So we're just kind of starting
to wrap our heads around that

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:27.220
and see how we can
take advantage of that.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:29.720
But we've already set
up a few custom metrics,

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:31.239
and we're looking
at the dashboards

00:20:31.239 --> 00:20:33.030
there and starting to
see-- it's definitely

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:34.780
really exciting
technology, and we're just

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:36.540
starting to figure
out how we can use it.

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:37.456
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:37.456 --> 00:20:39.520
Yeah, I was totally
thinking about Stackdriver,

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.270
when you said it's
like, yeah, it pings me

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:42.260
in the middle of the night.

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:43.426
We can do that as a service.

00:20:43.426 --> 00:20:45.125
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:45.125 --> 00:20:47.125
MARK MANDEL: Midnight
wake-up call as a service.

00:20:47.125 --> 00:20:48.344
I like it.

00:20:48.344 --> 00:20:49.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:50.790
So could you
mention-- you already

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:53.940
talked about what were
the hardest things.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.200
What about the best thing?

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:56.824
What is the moment
where you said, wow,

00:20:56.824 --> 00:20:59.740
it's cool to be
running on the cloud?

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:02.310
ANDY OXFELD: Yeah, so there's
definitely a number of those.

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:05.860
I mean, it's really cool when
we launch a bundle and you just,

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:09.240
as you mentioned, there's a live
calendar of how many people are

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:12.070
purchasing at the
bottom, and just

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:13.660
seeing all these
people coming in

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:17.479
and thinking about just how
many requests are being handled.

00:21:17.479 --> 00:21:19.645
And the fact that we didn't
have to worry about that

00:21:19.645 --> 00:21:22.830
is just an amazing thing.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:25.760
It's also really
satisfying when we've

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:28.690
done some cool
optimization tasks

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:32.590
we've done that have found
ways of making our site faster.

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:35.600
It's really cool
when sometimes we've

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.794
have pages that are taking
a little longer to load,

00:21:37.794 --> 00:21:39.210
and we've realized,
oh, hey, if we

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:40.876
use the datastore in
this different way,

00:21:40.876 --> 00:21:42.860
or if we add some
Memcache on top of this,

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:45.060
and we get down to
100 milliseconds.

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:46.320
So those have been cool.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:49.910
I have a question that
is super technical,

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:52.210
but I'm very curious about
how you're doing this.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:55.470
So I'm looking at the Humble
Bundle webpage right now,

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:57.470
