00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:31.606
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:31.606 --> 00:24:35.200
So let's go with the question
of the week, which is, as we

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:37.490
said before, about Kubernetes.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:42.860
And it came through Google+
from one of our previous

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:43.910
interviewees.

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:45.780
MARK MANDEL: Yes, and
very active commenter.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:47.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very, very active.

00:24:47.290 --> 00:24:47.790
Hi Heron.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:49.750
We know you're hearing.

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:51.245
You're listening to us.

00:24:51.245 --> 00:24:53.620
MARK MANDEL: So Heron said us
a really cool question, one

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.036
that I've definitely
heard before,

00:24:55.036 --> 00:24:57.870
a discussion around the fact
that Kubernetes 1.2 doesn't

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.550
have any idea of Cron.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.050
So if you want to, say, set
up a scheduled task that

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:05.130
does something on a regular
basis, say once a day

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:07.110
or once an hour,
something like that,

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:08.860
Kubernetes doesn't
have anything built in.

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:10.370
So how do you end up doing that?

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:11.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I guess that it

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:14.640
is important to
mention that it's not

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.410
part of Kubernetes as
1.2, but it's planned

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:19.540
to be released with 1.3.

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:20.840
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:22.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah,
what do you do then?

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:25.570
MARK MANDEL: So I think you
have a couple of options there.

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:28.730
One of maybe-- maybe the
most basic one is you

00:25:28.730 --> 00:25:32.360
can set up a Cron just on a
GCE instance, one of the nodes

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:35.650
on Kubernetes just using
standard Linux Cron,

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:37.730
running the commands
and doing that stuff,

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:39.640
and to set up a job.

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:42.040
Now, actually I'll backtrack
for a minute and say,

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.340
Kubernetes does have
a concept of jobs.

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.700
So if you have a
one-off task or one-off

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:49.232
process you want to run, have
it be managed by Kubernetes,

00:25:49.232 --> 00:25:51.190
make sure it gets finished,
that sort of stuff,

00:25:51.190 --> 00:25:53.330
jobs are definitely a
thing you need to look at.

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:54.746
But what you'd
want to do is you'd

00:25:54.746 --> 00:25:57.644
want to fire off that
job on a regular basis.

00:25:57.644 --> 00:25:59.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so jobs, I'd say,

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:00.879
jobs is more like
things like map

00:26:00.879 --> 00:26:02.670
reduce and stuff like
that, where you have,

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:05.375
these need to be
processed at some point.

00:26:05.375 --> 00:26:05.875
Just do it.

00:26:05.875 --> 00:26:06.583
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:26:06.583 --> 00:26:07.220
Yeah, exactly.

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:09.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, but so you're

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.170
saying that basically you
could start one of those jobs

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:15.844
through a call down
from an instance,

00:26:15.844 --> 00:26:17.619
a GCE instance running Cron.

00:26:17.619 --> 00:26:19.410
MARK MANDEL: Running
Cron, that would work.

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:20.402
That would be OK.

00:26:20.402 --> 00:26:22.110
But there's actually
nothing stopping you

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:24.127
from using the Cron
ability within App Engine.

00:26:24.127 --> 00:26:26.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I actually
like that much better.

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:27.500
MARK MANDEL: I like
this much better, too.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.190
Obviously then it's
a managed service.

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:31.910
You absolutely know it's
never going to go down.

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:34.045
Like if that GCE
instance died in a ball

00:26:34.045 --> 00:26:35.960
of fire, that would be bad.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.370
And you may not know, or
you'd need monitoring.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:38.870
But

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:41.020
Kubernetes has an
API, so you can set it

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:45.480
up so that you can fire off a
job using the API from at App

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:46.980
Engine, and then
you can probably

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.580
feel a whole lot happier
about it that it's happening

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:50.820
and it's always going to
happen at the time it's

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:51.830
meant to happen.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:53.050
I think that's my favorite.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:53.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:56.150
Also, the thing that I like
about this solution is that

00:26:56.150 --> 00:27:00.240
it's-- so Cron on App Engine
is based on task queues,

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:02.850
which means that
if, for any reason,

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:05.510
the request that you're sending
fails, it will retry it.

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:06.260
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:07.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
it's also a bonus.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:09.100
You're basically
just winning there.

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.990
I think it even comes with
exponential backoff and all

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:14.280
of those really fancy
things if you need to.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.370
So it's very easy to set up.

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:17.500
And it's very reliable.

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:19.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
yeah, absolutely.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.010
So I think that's
definitely the way I'd go,

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:23.830
especially obviously running
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.190
I think that's an easy solution.

00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:28.824
And with the scale to zero
of the extended App Engine,

00:27:28.824 --> 00:27:30.490
you're not paying for
very much, either.

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:31.406
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:31.406 --> 00:27:31.990
Cool.

